\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate SICA against Self-Consistency (SC) across six models spanning three base architectures (Qwen, Mistral, LLaMA), including instruction-tuned, reasoning-specialized, and distilled variants, and three reasoning domains, with two additional benchmarks as operating-regime probes.

\paragraph{Models.} We test Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\cite{yang2024qwen25}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct~\cite{dubey2024llama3}, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\cite{guo2025deepseekr1}, and QwQ-32B~\cite{qwen2024qwq}, spanning 7B to 32B parameters. The Qwen2.5-7B condition enables within-family scaling comparison.

\paragraph{Datasets.} FOLIO~\cite{han2022folio} (validation split) provides formal logic problems with gold FOL annotations ($n=204$), enabling the oracle control (\S\ref{sec:oracle}). ProofWriter~\cite{tafjord2021proofwriter} tests multi-step deductive reasoning ($n=600$). StrategyQA~\cite{geva2021strategyqa} evaluates commonsense reasoning ($n=200$ for LLaMA-3.1-8B; $n=60$ for other models). ARC-Challenge~\cite{clark2018arc} covers science questions ($n=60$). GSM8K~\cite{cobbe2021gsm8k} ($n=30$) and AIME 2024 ($n=22$) serve as operating-regime probes, both exhibiting near-ceiling SC accuracy or near-zero $C_0$.

\paragraph{Parameters.} We sample $K=12$ traces per question at temperature $T=0.7$. Z3~\cite{demoura2008z3} solves each MAX-SAT instance with a 10-second timeout. Temperature ablation varies $T \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$.

\paragraph{Metrics.} We report accuracy (\%), $C_0$ (\S\ref{sec:c0}), and $\Delta = \text{SICA} - \text{SC}$ in percentage points (pp). Statistical significance uses the McNemar test at $\alpha = 0.05$. For conditions with $n \geq 200$, we compute 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals with 10{,}000 resamples.


\subsection{Main Results: SICA Provides No Significant Gain}
\label{sec:main-results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports results across all model-domain conditions. Symbolic constraint aggregation does not improve over majority voting.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Accuracy of Self-Consistency (SC) and SICA across three primary domains and two supplementary probes. $\Delta$ denotes the SICA$-$SC gap in percentage points. No self-extraction condition reaches statistical significance ($\alpha = 0.05$). The oracle upper bound replaces self-extracted constraints with gold FOL annotations verified by Z3. $\dagger$: $n \leq 60$, insufficient power for confirmatory conclusions. $\ddagger$: $n=30$ pilot, included for operating regime characterization. $^a$McNemar test for $n \geq 200$; exact binomial test on discordant pairs for $n \leq 60$.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Domain & $n$ & SC (\%) & SICA (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p^a$ \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 204 & 79.41 & 79.41 & 0.00 & --- \\
Qwen2.5-14B & ProofWriter & 600 & 75.17 & 75.50 & $+$0.33 & n.s. \\
Qwen2.5-14B & StrategyQA$^\dagger$ & 60 & 53.33 & 56.67 & $+$3.33 & 0.50 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & ARC-Chall.$^\dagger$ & 60 & 96.67 & 95.00 & $-$1.67 & --- \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO+PW$^\dagger$ & 56 & 69.64 & 69.64 & 0.00 & 1.000 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO+PW$^\dagger$ & 60 & 48.33 & 48.33 & 0.00 & 1.000 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & StrategyQA & 200 & 72.00 & 74.00 & $+$2.00 & 0.424 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & FOLIO & 204 & 63.24 & 60.78 & $-$2.45 & 0.267 \\
DeepSeek-R1-7B & FOLIO+PW$^\dagger$ & 60 & 71.67 & 68.33 & $-$3.33 & 0.480 \\
QwQ-32B & AIME$^\dagger$ & 22 & 72.70 & 72.70 & 0.00 & --- \\
Qwen2.5-14B & GSM8K$^\ddagger$ & 30 & 96.67 & 96.67 & 0.00 & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Oracle upper bound (gold FOL + Z3 replaces self-extraction)}} \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 204 & 79.41 & 88.24 & $+$8.82 & $<$0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Across the four adequately powered conditions ($n \geq 200$, combined $N = 1{,}208$), $\Delta$ ranges from $-2.45$ to $+2.00$~pp, with no condition reaching significance ($p = 0.267$ and $0.424$ for the two nonzero gaps). The seven smaller conditions ($n \leq 60$) are consistent with this null pattern: $\Delta$ ranges from $-3.33$ to $+3.33$~pp, all nonsignificant. QwQ-32B on AIME produces identical outputs ($C_0 = 2.6\%$), confirming that reasoning-specialized models leave no room for SICA to operate.

Across eleven conditions, six models, and more than 1,500 instances (four confirmatory conditions with $n \geq 200$, seven exploratory conditions with $n \leq 60$), SICA does not improve upon majority voting. The oracle row tells a different story: gold FOL constraints yield $+8.82$~pp over SC ($p < 0.001$), proving the aggregation mechanism works when given discriminative constraints.


\subsection{Oracle Control: The Aggregation Mechanism Is Effective}
\label{sec:oracle}

We construct an oracle variant that replaces self-extracted constraints with gold FOL annotations from FOLIO ($n = 204$). Z3 evaluates ground-truth formulas against each candidate answer; all other pipeline components remain identical.

Oracle-SICA achieves 88.24\% accuracy versus 79.41\% for both SC and standard SICA. The $+8.82$~pp gain is highly significant (McNemar $p = 6.15 \times 10^{-5}$). Z3 checks on gold annotations agree with ground-truth labels for 193/204 problems (94.6\%). This establishes that the MAX-SAT mechanism is sound given correct constraints, and that the bottleneck lies entirely in extraction quality.


\subsection{Capability Trap Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Why does SICA fail? Full per-condition metrics are in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:capability_trap}. $C_0$ ranges from 2.6\% (QwQ-32B) to 61.8\% (LLaMA-3.1-8B on FOLIO). For reasoning-specialized models, over 97\% of questions receive unanimous answers, so SICA reduces to SC by construction. For instruction-tuned models, higher $C_0$ creates opportunities for SICA to differ from SC, yet $\Delta$ remains nonsignificant everywhere (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostics}b). Extraction rates exceed 0.80 and contradiction rates stay below 7\%, so the pipeline is mechanically functional. The problem is extraction vacuity: constraints are syntactically valid but semantically redundant with their source answers. Even the highest-quality condition (LLaMA-3.1-8B on FOLIO, extraction rate 0.94) yields $\Delta = -2.45$~pp. A temperature ablation ($T \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$, Qwen2.5-14B, $n{=}60$) shows $C_0$ increasing monotonically from 30.0\% ($T{=}0.3$) to 38.3\% ($T{=}0.5$), 40.0\% ($T{=}0.7$), and 43.3\% ($T{=}1.0$), yet $\Delta$ never exceeds zero: 0.0~pp at $T{=}0.3$, 0.0~pp at $T{=}0.5$, $-3.33$~pp at $T{=}0.7$ ($n{=}60$, n.s.), and 0.0~pp at $T{=}1.0$ (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostics}a). Even at the default temperature $T{=}0.7$, SICA does not outperform SC.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_capability_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Vote entropy vs.\ constraint exclusion rate (Qwen2.5-14B, $n{=}307$ multi-candidate problems).}
\label{fig:capability_scatter}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig3_operating_regime.pdf}
\caption{$C_0$ vs.\ SICA$-$SC gap across all conditions.}
\label{fig:operating_regime}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Capability trap diagnostics. (a) High vote entropy does not benefit from constraint filtering. (b) The accuracy gap stays centered at zero regardless of answer diversity.}
\label{fig:diagnostics}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Constraint Ablation: MAX-SAT Degenerates to Counting}
\label{sec:ablation}

If self-extracted constraints are non-discriminative, MAX-SAT should produce the same predictions as baselines ignoring satisfiability. We test three variants on Qwen2.5-14B (FOLIO + ProofWriter, $n = 804$): full SICA, count-only (most constraints wins, ignoring satisfiability), and random-weight (randomized weights before solving).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Constraint ablation (Qwen2.5-14B, $n = 804$, FOLIO + ProofWriter). Agreement: predictions identical to SC out of 804.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrc@{}}
\toprule
Aggregation & Acc.\ (\%) & Agreement w/ SC \\
\midrule
SC (majority vote) & 76.24 & --- \\
SICA (MAX-SAT) & 76.49 & 773\,/\,804 \\
Count-only & 76.62 & 774\,/\,804 \\
Random-weight & 75.87 & 764\,/\,804 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 803 of 804 predictions, confirming that the satisfiability solver contributes nothing beyond vote counting (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). Against SC, all three variants disagree on 30--40 predictions (773--774 for SICA and count-only, 764 for random-weight), but wins and losses cancel: among SICA's 31 discordant pairs with SC, 16 favor SICA and 14 favor SC, yielding a net gain of only $+$2 problems. Random-weight deviates most from SC (764/804) and achieves the lowest accuracy (75.87\%), confirming that randomizing constraint weights introduces noise without discriminative benefit. Self-extracted constraints carry no signal beyond vote counts. The oracle control ($+8.82$~pp) confirms that the aggregation mechanism works given independent problem structure; self-extracted constraints simply fail to provide it.

